{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"2953","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-github-contents-http-replay-tests.md@sha256:61bb0c8375ac76ab0a0a5dd225ec4a24292856b827fb5cd4cc12094fad8f7e45"},"owner_reference":"2953","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nReplace `github_contents.py`'s Protocol-shim test doubles with PyGithub HTTP-layer replay testing\n\n## Problem Statement\n\n`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_contents.py` defines 7 Protocol shims (`_ContentsFile`, `_Commit`, `_Branch`, `_GitTreeEntry`, `_GitTree`, `_Requester`, `_ContentsRepository`) so hand-rolled Pydantic/custom test doubles can structurally satisfy PyGithub's real return types, because those real classes (`Repository`, `ContentFile`, `Commit`, `Branch`, `GitTree`, etc.) have private, Requester-bound constructors a test double cannot nominally subclass.\n\nThe current test suite (`plugins/development-harness/tests/test_github_contents.py`) mocks entirely at the `Repository`/`Requester` method level \u2014 no HTTP interception is involved. Tests never exercise genuine PyGithub objects built from real API response shapes, meaning a PyGithub response-shape change, or a subtly wrong hand-rolled double, can silently diverge from real API behavior undetected.\n\nInvestigation (2026-08-18) confirmed PyGithub's production code (`Requester.py`) routes 100% of its HTTP calls through the `requests` library with no alternate transport path, and that PyGithub does not publish its own `ReplayData`/`Framework.TestCase` test utilities for external reuse \u2014 any HTTP-layer replay harness adopted here would be custom-built using a standard library such as `vcrpy` or `responses`.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. Tests exercise genuine PyGithub objects (`Repository`, `ContentFile`, `Commit`, `Branch`, `GitTree`, etc.) built from real or realistically recorded API responses, not hand-rolled Protocol-shim doubles.\n2. The number of custom Protocol shims in `github_contents.py` needed purely to satisfy test-double construction is reduced or eliminated.\n3. The test suite remains fast and deterministic \u2014 no live network calls during normal CI runs.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. Not a live-integration test suite \u2014 no requirement for tests to call the real GitHub API during normal CI runs.\n2. Not a rewrite of `github_contents.py`'s production logic \u2014 scope is limited to the test-doubling strategy.\n3. Not a decision on which specific library (`vcrpy` vs `responses` vs a custom cassette recorder) \u2014 that is a design/planning question, not settled here.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. The test suite for `github_contents.py` must be able to construct real PyGithub `Repository`/`ContentFile`/`Commit`/`Branch`/`GitTree` objects for use in assertions, rather than only Protocol-satisfying fakes.\n2. Wherever HTTP-layer replay is adopted, recorded fixtures (cassettes or mocked response payloads) must not require live GitHub credentials to run in CI.\n3. Existing test coverage and assertions must be preserved or improved \u2014 no loss of test coverage during migration.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. No new runtime (non-dev) dependency is added to `backlog_core`'s PEP 723 script metadata \u2014 any new test library (`vcrpy`/`responses`) is a dev-only/test-only dependency, consistent with this repo's \"no uv workspace\" convention (root `pyproject.toml [dependency-groups] dev`).\n2. Test suite execution time should not regress meaningfully versus the current in-memory-fake approach.\n3. No live network access is required for normal `uv run pytest` runs (cassette replay or mocked responses only).\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\n1. User proposed `vcrpy` or `responses` as HTTP-layer recorder/interceptor libraries. Investigation confirms both are structurally compatible with PyGithub's `requests`-based transport (no known incompatibility with PyGithub's retry/session pooling), but the choice between them \u2014 and between real GitHub-recorded cassettes vs. hand-authored response matchers \u2014 is a design decision for planning, not settled here.\n2. Whether to migrate all 7 identified Protocol shims to real-object-based tests, or only a subset \u2014 a fidelity-vs-migration-cost trade-off deferred to planning.\n3. Three viable implementation paths were identified during investigation (see References): (A) HTTP-layer cassettes recorded once against the live GitHub API and replayed in CI; (B) cassette-free `responses`-based request matchers; (C) hybrid \u2014 keep current in-memory fakes as the default, use `vcrpy` only for cassette recording/refresh during development. Path selection is deferred to planning/architecture.\n\n## References\n\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_contents.py:79-138` \u2014 the 7 Protocol shim definitions\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_github_contents.py:31-206` \u2014 current test double implementations\n- PR #2939 / commits `899c9b20`, `90c4abba` \u2014 prior documentation of the Protocol-shim rationale\n- Installed PyGithub 2.9.0 `Requester.py` (site-packages) \u2014 confirms `requests`-based HTTP transport with no alternate path\n- `vcrpy`, `responses` \u2014 candidate HTTP-layer interception libraries; both compatible per investigation, neither currently a declared dependency\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Does `github_contents.py`'s test setup route PyGithub calls through `requests` in a way `vcrpy` could intercept? | Production PyGithub code routes 100% of HTTP through `requests` (confirmed via `Requester.py` import inspection), and `vcrpy`/`responses` can intercept at that layer with no known blocking incompatibility. However, the CURRENT test suite bypasses HTTP entirely \u2014 it mocks at the `Repository`/`Requester` method level, not the request level \u2014 so adopting HTTP-layer replay requires switching tests to construct real PyGithub objects, not merely adding `vcrpy` on top of the existing fakes. | `dh:codebase-analyzer` investigation, 2026-08-18, via direct inspection of installed PyGithub 2.9.0 source and this repo's `test_github_contents.py` |\n| Is `vcrpy` or `responses` already a dependency, and would adding one conflict with repo conventions? | Neither is currently declared anywhere in the repo. Either could be added as a dev-only dependency in root `pyproject.toml [dependency-groups] dev` without conflicting with the \"no uv workspace\" / PEP 723 self-resolving script convention, since it is test infrastructure, not a runtime dependency of `backlog_core` itself. | Same investigation |\n| Does PyGithub publish its own `Framework.TestCase` / `ReplayData` test utilities for reuse? | No \u2014 PyGithub's published package does not expose those internal test utilities to consumers. Any HTTP-layer replay harness here must be custom-built (using `vcrpy`/`responses`), not reused directly from PyGithub. | Same investigation |\n| How many Protocol shims actually exist in `github_contents.py`? | 7 were found (`_ContentsFile`, `_Commit`, `_Branch`, `_GitTreeEntry`, `_GitTree`, `_Requester`, `_ContentsRepository`), not the 5 stated in the original item description \u2014 a minor factual discrepancy for the fact-checker/groomer to confirm during the swarm pass. | Same investigation, `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_contents.py:79-138` |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- None that block proceeding to planning. All genuine unknowns identified in the original item were resolved by investigation. The choice of specific implementation path (A/B/C above) is a design question routed to planning, not a blocker.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [x] No unbound constraints, domain-knowledge gap, or contradictory requirements were found during discovery. Design questions are routed to planning per the human-touchpoint gate rather than escalated. Session mode is interactive; user has signaled a general preference for autonomous progress \u2014 discovery proceeds without a separate confirmation round-trip. User remains free to redirect at the next touchpoint (groom output review or planning)."}